TL;DR

- Self-hosting Hi.Events is free, and these files can stay in your install.
- Using the features they add, like reserved seating and the box office, for
  real events needs a licence: https://hi.events/licensing
- Development and testing are always free.
- You can share, fork and modify this code as part of Hi.Events, but don't
  remove or bypass the licence key checks.
- If your licence ends, you can keep running event dates that were already
  scheduled. You just can't set up anything new.
- If you have a signed licence agreement with us, that agreement comes first.

This summary is for convenience. Where it differs from the terms below, the
terms apply. Questions? hello@hi.events


TERMS

1. Definitions. "We" and "us" mean Hi.Events Ltd. "The Software" means the
   files in this directory and its subdirectories, in source or compiled
   form, including modified versions of them. "Licensed Features" are the
   Hi.Events features that require a licence key, including reserved seating
   and the box office. A "Commercial Licence" is a licence to use Licensed
   Features in production that we have issued to you, as a written agreement
   or a licence key. "Production" means running real events, for example
   selling or issuing tickets or admitting attendees.

2. Permission. Subject to these terms, you may use, copy, modify and
   distribute the Software, but only as part of Hi.Events or a modified
   version of it, and only if you keep these terms with it. You may not
   sublicense the Software or sell it. Anyone who receives the Software from
   you needs their own Commercial Licence to use Licensed Features in
   production.

3. Modifications. You may modify the Software and publish patches to it. You
   agree that we retain all right, title and interest in and to all such
   modifications and patches, and that they may only be used, copied,
   modified and distributed under these terms.

4. Licence required. Using a Licensed Feature in production requires a
   Commercial Licence that covers that feature, and you must stay within
   that licence's limits. This applies whether you run Hi.Events for
   yourself or on behalf of others. A licence from us for something else,
   such as removing the "Powered by Hi.Events" attribution, does not cover
   Licensed Features unless it says so. Running Hi.Events in production with
   Licensed Features switched off does not require a Commercial Licence.

5. Development and testing. You may use Licensed Features without a
   Commercial Licence for development and testing, but not in production.

6. Licence keys and notices. You may not remove, disable or work around the
   licence key checks, distribute a version of the Software in which they
   have been removed, disabled or worked around, or remove any licensing or
   copyright notices.

7. When a Commercial Licence ends. You may keep using the Software to run
   event dates that were already scheduled when your Commercial Licence
   ended, and to wind down what you set up under it, but not to set up
   anything new.

8. Commercial agreements. If you have a signed Commercial Licence agreement
   with us, its terms apply in place of these terms wherever the two differ.

9. Termination. Your rights under these terms end automatically if you
   breach them. They are restored if you fix the breach within 30 days of
   first becoming aware of it.

10. Other rights. You are not granted any rights beyond those expressly
    stated in these terms. For all third-party components incorporated into
